<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arlon</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arlon is a town in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, close to the border with the Grand Duchy. It grew from the Roman settlement of Orolaunum on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of counts whose house founded the County of Limburg, and the lordship of Arlon later passed to Luxembourg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arlon.jpg" alt="Arlon" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Pol GRANDMONT · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arlon_E2aJPG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Roman conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, the area around Arlon belonged to the Treveri, a Celtic tribe. The local people adapted relatively easily to Roman culture, and the vicus of Orolaunum quickly became a commercial and administrative centre. The Germanic invasions of the 3rd century destroyed most of these gains despite the defensive walls built on the Knipchen hill.</p><p>In 1060 Waleran I of Limburg, Count of Arlon, built a castle on the Knipchen hill. When Duke Waleran III died in 1226, Arlon passed to his son Henry V the Fair, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, and became part of the county of Luxembourg. In 1558 French troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-duke-of-guise">Francis, Duke of Guise</a>, destroyed nearly half the city, castle included. In the 17th century Capuchin friars built a convent on the castle's ruins, and a fire destroyed much of the city in 1785. On 9 June 1793 French Revolutionary troops defeated the Austrians outside it and took the city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> of 1839 the western part of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, the province of Luxembourg with Arlon, went to the new Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. Arlon is the capital of that province and the smallest provincial capital in Belgium, with just over 28,000 people. On 26 August 1914, 121 inhabitants were executed on the orders of Colonel Richard Karl von Tessmar in the German invasion.</p><p>It holds one of the richest archaeological museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, with Roman sculpture and Merovingian funerary art. A fragment of the Gallo-Roman wall of the 3rd century still stands, and the church of Saint Donat occupies the Knipchen hill. The carnival takes place at mid-Lent, and the Maitrank, white wine in which a local flower, Asperula odorata, has macerated, is the city's most popular drink.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arlon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The castle of Limbourg on the Vesdre was built in the early 11th century, and around 1065 Walram Udo of the house of <b>Arlon</b> became count of Limburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-limburg">Duchy of Limburg</a></li></ul></section>
